Announcing "bsd games" version 1.0
----------------------------------

No unix system is truly complete without the traditional bsd games such as
battlestar, canfield, fish, fortune, hangman, mille, robots, wump, etc., etc.

So, not being able to find more than a few of these scattered around,
Andy Tefft and I (Curt Olson) have taken it upon ourselves to port the whole
collection to Linux.  

We have uploaded our initial release to the incoming directories at
tsx-11.mit.edu and sunsite.unc.edu.

They should eventually find their way to 
/pub/linux/source/usr.games/bsd-games.src.tar.gz and 
/pub/linux/binaries/usr.games/bsd-games.bin.tar.gz on tsx-11.mit.edu
and
/pub/Linux/games/[bsd-games.src.tar.gz,bsd-games.bin.tar.gz] on sunsite.unc.edu

Enclosed is the README.linux file from this distribution:

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
8/17/93

Here is version 1.0 of our bsdgames package, containing ports of 
your favorite curses-based bsd games (descriptions to follow).
These games mostly came from ftp.uu.net:/systems/unix/bsd-sources/games 
with some older versions from the Tahoe release. There's not much to do in
porting most of them, mostly busy-work. We didn't really try to enhance
them. Maybe later. We wanted to get out what we thought were the most
popular games first so we will eventually hopefully have the whole
bunch done (If you want to help, contact us!).

They should work with curses or ncurses. Each subdirectory has a README.linux
which should describe how to change from the default curses to ncurses.

