Subject: SERIOUS swap bug in SLS 1.03
Followup-To: comp.os.linux.help
Date: 27 Aug 1993 14:47:12 GMT
Organization: Linux. It's not just for breakfast anymore.
Lines: 22
Approved: linux-announce@tc.cornell.edu (Matt Welsh)
Message-ID: <25l6pg$53j@samba.oit.unc.edu>
NNTP-Posting-Host: calypso.oit.unc.edu
Keywords: SLS, bug, problem

It has been brought to my attention that there is a serious configuration
bug with SLS 1.03 regarding swap space. For some reason, /dev/swap is
a soft link to /dev/hda1 regardless of the swap space configuration. 
Because of this, anyone who attempts to "dd" or "mkswap" on /dev/swap will
wipe their /dev/hda1 partition. (Unless your swap partition is /dev/hda1
this will hose something for sure.) 

You should not use the "/dev/swap" device on SLS 1.03. To initialize
swap space, you should use "mkswap <device> <size>" where <device> is the
partition used for swap, such as /dev/hda2. Some of these "conventions"
adopted by SLS are not only nonstandard but are obviously causing some
confusion for new users. Your best bet: IGNORE the /dev/swap device.
It shouldn't be there anyway and is apparently an attempt to make SLS
easier to use, albeit the fact that it is not a standard device.

Thanks to cgs@sg1.psu.edu (Carl G. Schott) for bringing this to my
attention.

mdw

