In May we announced plans to start Linux Journal, a magazine for the
Linux community.  This is the status report.  The executive summary is,
"oh well, good try".

I am not the sort of person who likes to promise and not deliver so I am
not enjoying writing this but here goes.

Basically, an amazing set of bad happenings stomped on all efforts to get
LJ going.  The highlights include:
   * a close friend, Linux activist, person doing some of the work to
     get LJ started and only serious investor other than myself died
   * the editor deciding she really couldn't do the job of editor
   * a financial deal that was supposed to net me a bunch of cash
     to invest didn't happen
   * a vendor welched on an agreement we had about mailing list use

There is more but this is enough.  Those of us involved basically lost
momentum and not getting stuff done on time means things like the
advertising manager (who had 3 months to get stuff started) will
soon be off with another committment.

I don't think any of the things that happened suggest that LJ isn't
a good idea.  I feel as strongly as ever that this needs to be done.
The more I work with the Linux community and "future linux community"
the more I feel a publication is required.  For example, I spoke to
the Seattle UNIX Group about Linux.  The room was packed, questions
were there in quantity and enthusiasm was high.  My guess is that
most of the 60 or 70 people in the room would have gladly subscribed
to LJ to help them get up to speed.

If there is anyone out there (well, any company) interested in attempting
to get this publication going I would be willing to work with them.
I have a lot of time and money invested at this point and would still
like to see it happen but don't have enough of either time or money
to make it work.

If you are intested in "talking about this", e-mail to this account,
mail to Linux Journal, P.O. Box 85867, Seattle, WA 98145-1867,
a FAX to (206)526-0803 or, somewhat less desirably as I have a lot
of "cleanup" to do and would prefer not to have to respond in real time,
, a phone call to (206)526-2919 is in order.

Finally I would like to thank all those who responded to our questionnaire
that was posted back in May.  The response was overwhelmingly positive
and included a lot of good suggestions.

Phil Hughes, Publisher, (the unpublished) Linux Journal

