I have uploaded Linux binaries of version 2.05 of Lout, the
typesetter, to sunsite.unc.edu and tsx-11.mit.edu.

Lout is a typesetting language written by Jeffrey Kingston at the
University of Sidney.  It is almost totally unlike TeX or LaTeX.  I
found it easier (possible!) to learn and program, and it produces very
good looking output as a small, well-structured PostScript file.

The main new feature in 2.05 is support for ISO8859 8-bit characters.
Using this and Emacs 19 you should be able to just type your national
characters and have them printed with no special bother.

If you want an example of the output, the PostScript version of the
new Linux FAQ was done in Lout.

Read the file lout-2.05-bin.README for installation instructions and
for my digital signature of the tarfile.

You'll need about 3.85Mb of free space; if you can live without the
formatted (PostScript) copies of the manual you'll save around 1.7Mb.

The executable is linked against libc.so.4.4.1.


